※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ **Formatting takes room from an arena it is told about**, and the print functions tell it
※ about a pool they empty when they are done.
※
※ `std.text` takes a reference to the arena and every join inside it goes there; `⍕v in a` is
※ the operator form of the same thing, which is what `std.format` writes into the code it
※ generates.  So a program that prints a formatted line declares **nothing**: the device
※ carries permission to write and the pool carries permission to allocate.
※
※ `std.printing` is that pool, and it is emptied at the end of every print -- so nothing may
※ be kept in it.  Text that is to last comes from `std.format`, which takes room from `⎕heap`.

let std := ⎕import("std")

type Point = x: u8 ; y: i16

※ A pipe, which is the one thing here that needs `@[impure]`: a raw system call may do
※ anything, so nothing about it is accounted for.
@[impure]
fn piped() → std.Writer:
    let ends: mut i32⟦2⟧ = ⟦0i32, 0i32⟧
    if ⎕syscall(⎕sc@pipe2, &mut ends⟦0⟧, 0i64) < 0i64:
        std.Writer(.fd ← ⁻1i32)
    else:
        std.Writer(.fd ← ends⟦1⟧)

※ And the printing, which declares nothing at all: the device carries permission to
※ write and the pool carries permission to allocate.
fn said(out: &mut std.Writer) → u6:
    ※ A template with no holes: no join at all, so no room is taken anywhere.
    if ⎕drop(std.println⌜out, "plain"⌝ ?? 0) ≠ 6u64:
        return 2u6
    ※ And one with holes, whose text is built in the pool and given back after the write.
    ※ The answer is bytes and not characters: `←` and `⁻` take three and two.
    let shaped: Point = Point(.x ← 1u8, .y ← ⁻2i16)
    if ⎕drop(std.println⌜out, "{} {}", 42u16, shaped⌝ ?? 0) ≠ 32u64:
        return 3u6
    0u6

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let out: mut std.Writer = piped()
    if out.fd < 0i32:
        return 1u6
    let how: u6 = said(&mut out)
    if how ≠ 0u6:
        return how

    0u6
